<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- botato is signed with a Developer ID and notarised, not sandboxed: it
       spawns the Claude Code CLI and a container engine, and reads a workspace
       the user can open in Finder. None of that survives the App Sandbox.

       Kept deliberately short. Every entitlement is a claim to be justified at
       review, and an unnecessary one is a claim that cannot be.

       The hardened runtime needs no exception for spawning child processes; each
       child carries its own signature. What it does forbid is loading unsigned
       code into our own process, which botato never does. -->

  <!-- The webview JITs JavaScript. On macOS that happens in Apple's own
       WebContent process, but this is inherited by children and costs nothing
       to state. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- The microphone, for calls and for dictating into the composer. Under the
       hardened runtime an app is refused protected resources unless it says
       which ones it wants, so this is what makes "hold to talk" and the mic
       button able to hear anything. It is a claim we can justify: nothing
       listens except between a press and the press that stops it. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
